<PageShell title="Reflection on Course Implementation">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Teaching asynchronously requires me to think differently about instructional presence. In a physical
      classroom, I can immediately see whether students are confused, ask spontaneous questions, adjust my
      explanation, or remind students what they should focus on. In an asynchronous course, I do not have
      access to those visual cues. I therefore have to intentionally build instructional presence into the
      course.
    </p>
    <p>
      I do this through multiple forms of communication and instruction. Students encounter biological
      concepts through readings, short instructional videos, optional videos, adaptive learning activities,
      labs, study guides, quizzes, and application assignments. Important information is also intentionally
      repeated across locations within the course. For example, expectations appear in an announcement as
      well as the module overview. This redundancy is deliberate: students may overlook information in one
      location, but encounter it again elsewhere.
    </p>
    <p>
      My preference for shorter instructional videos—generally under ten minutes—was initially based on the
      belief that students are more likely to complete shorter videos. However, student feedback has caused
      me to reconsider whether this approach should apply equally to all concepts. Some biological
      processes require more sustained explanation. I therefore plan to add longer instructor-created videos
      selectively for challenging concepts while continuing to use shorter videos for foundational material.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also use the structure of assignments to promote active learning: adaptive learning provides repeated
      opportunities for practice, while application assignments ask students to use biological concepts in
      authentic contexts. Labs provide opportunities for students to engage in scientific processes, and the
      study guides help direct their attention toward essential concepts.
    </p>
    <p>
      One of the most rewarding aspects of teaching these courses has been seeing students bring their own
      experiences into the learning process. For example, during an invertebrate presentation, one student
      incorporated a personal experience of being stung by a jellyfish into a presentation about their
      selected organism. This type of connection demonstrates the potential of asynchronous assignments to
      become personally meaningful rather than simply transactional. In future iterations, where appropriate,
      I plan to explicitly invite students to incorporate relevant personal experiences into their work.
    </p>
  </div>
</PageShell>
